1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:02,080
This machine runs at about ninety

2
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:04,320
decibels. A quieter one might run at

3
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:06,320
eighty. That sounds like a small

4
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:08,720
difference. But it's actually ten times

5
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:10,160
the sound intensity.

6
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:12,150
Here's the math, and why it matters for

7
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:13,440
your hearing.

8
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:16,560
Let's see what a decibel really measures.

9
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:18,080
Sound carries energy.

10
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:20,400
Engineers measure sound intensity in watts

11
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:21,760
per square meter.

12
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:24,080
Under ideal conditions, the faintest sound

13
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:26,160
a healthy human ear can detect has an

14
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:28,480
intensity of about one trillionth of a

15
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:30,160
watt per square meter.

16
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:32,590
A jet taking off nearby can produce about

17
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:34,800
a hundred watts per square meter.

18
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:36,960
That's a hundred trillion times more

19
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:39,600
intense. A normal numerical scale would be

20
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:41,600
difficult to use across such an enormous

21
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:42,230
range.

22
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:44,560
So engineers use a logarithmic scale

23
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:47,680
instead. The sound intensity level, L, in

24
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:50,400
decibels, equals ten times the logarithm,

25
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:53,680
base ten, of I divided by I zero.

26
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:56,160
Here, I zero is the reference intensity:

27
00:00:56,160 --> 00:00:58,640
one trillionth of a watt per square meter.

28
00:00:58,850 --> 00:01:00,850
A logarithm tells us how many factors of

29
00:01:00,850 --> 00:01:02,210
ten are involved.

30
00:01:02,210 --> 00:01:04,130
Ten times the reference intensity gives

31
00:01:04,130 --> 00:01:04,880
ten decibels.

32
00:01:04,890 --> 00:01:07,570
A hundred times gives twenty decibels.

33
00:01:07,570 --> 00:01:09,320
And a trillion times gives one hundred and

34
00:01:09,330 --> 00:01:11,490
twenty decibels.

35
00:01:11,490 --> 00:01:13,730
Here's how everyday sounds compare.

36
00:01:13,890 --> 00:01:16,050
The reference threshold of human hearing

37
00:01:16,050 --> 00:01:17,650
is zero decibels.

38
00:01:17,650 --> 00:01:19,970
A soft whisper, around thirty decibels,

39
00:01:20,050 --> 00:01:21,730
has a thousand times the reference

40
00:01:21,730 --> 00:01:24,210
intensity. Normal conversation, around

41
00:01:24,210 --> 00:01:26,690
sixty, has a million times.

42
00:01:26,690 --> 00:01:28,600
A loud motorcycle engine, around ninety

43
00:01:28,610 --> 00:01:31,890
five, has roughly three billion times.

44
00:01:31,890 --> 00:01:33,960
And a jet taking off nearby, around a

45
00:01:33,970 --> 00:01:36,450
hundred and forty decibels, has a hundred

46
00:01:36,610 --> 00:01:38,050
trillion times.

47
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:40,290
These levels are approximate and depend on

48
00:01:40,290 --> 00:01:41,490
the source and distance.

49
00:01:41,490 --> 00:01:43,250
But the mathematical relationship is

50
00:01:43,250 --> 00:01:45,730
always the same: every thirty decibels

51
00:01:45,730 --> 00:01:48,370
represents another factor of a thousand.

52
00:01:48,370 --> 00:01:50,210
Now back to our machines.

53
00:01:50,210 --> 00:01:52,850
Going from eighty to ninety decibels adds

54
00:01:52,850 --> 00:01:55,410
ten. In the formula, ten more decibels

55
00:01:55,410 --> 00:01:57,410
means the logarithm increases by exactly

56
00:01:57,410 --> 00:01:59,730
one. And that means one additional factor

57
00:01:59,730 --> 00:02:02,940
of ten. So ninety decibels represents ten

58
00:02:02,940 --> 00:02:04,940
times the sound intensity of eighty.

59
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:06,620
Go up to a hundred decibels, and the

60
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:08,380
intensity is a hundred times that of the

61
00:02:08,380 --> 00:02:10,540
original eighty decibel sound.

62
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:12,140
Here's a smaller increase with a

63
00:02:12,140 --> 00:02:13,580
surprisingly big meaning.

64
00:02:13,580 --> 00:02:15,180
What happens when you double the

65
00:02:15,180 --> 00:02:17,500
intensity? The change in sound level is

66
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:19,660
ten times the logarithm of two.

67
00:02:19,660 --> 00:02:21,820
That's approximately three decibels.

68
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:23,980
So every three decibel increase nearly

69
00:02:23,980 --> 00:02:25,580
doubles the sound intensity.

70
00:02:25,580 --> 00:02:27,260
Going from eighty five to eighty eight

71
00:02:27,260 --> 00:02:29,740
decibels is approximately a doubling.

72
00:02:29,740 --> 00:02:32,140
What happens when two independent machines

73
00:02:32,140 --> 00:02:33,900
each produce eighty decibels at your

74
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:35,100
listening position?

75
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:37,020
You don't add the decibel numbers.

76
00:02:37,020 --> 00:02:39,100
You add their sound intensities.

77
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:41,420
Two machines produce twice the intensity,

78
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:43,740
giving approximately three decibels more.

79
00:02:43,740 --> 00:02:45,490
So eighty plus eighty makes about eighty

80
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:47,020
three decibels.

81
00:02:47,020 --> 00:02:49,580
Ten identical, independent machines, each

82
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:51,020
producing eighty decibels at that

83
00:02:51,020 --> 00:02:53,020
position, produce ten times the combined

84
00:02:53,020 --> 00:02:55,900
intensity of one machine: ninety decibels.

85
00:02:56,190 --> 00:02:58,830
But does ninety decibels sound ten times

86
00:02:58,830 --> 00:03:00,350
louder than eighty?

87
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:01,710
Not necessarily.

88
00:03:01,710 --> 00:03:03,580
Perceived loudness is different from

89
00:03:03,590 --> 00:03:05,550
physical sound intensity.

90
00:03:05,550 --> 00:03:08,190
For many everyday sounds, a ten decibel

91
00:03:08,190 --> 00:03:10,190
increase is perceived as roughly twice as

92
00:03:10,190 --> 00:03:12,670
loud. So the louder machine may sound

93
00:03:12,670 --> 00:03:14,670
about twice as loud, even though its

94
00:03:14,670 --> 00:03:16,990
intensity is ten times greater.

95
00:03:16,990 --> 00:03:18,910
That distinction becomes especially

96
00:03:18,910 --> 00:03:21,230
important when protecting your hearing.

97
00:03:21,230 --> 00:03:23,230
This is why workplace noise guidelines

98
00:03:23,390 --> 00:03:25,790
consider both sound level and exposure

99
00:03:25,790 --> 00:03:27,710
time. NIOSH, the U.S.

100
00:03:27,790 --> 00:03:29,790
agency that researches occupational safety

101
00:03:29,790 --> 00:03:31,790
and health, recommends an eight hour

102
00:03:31,790 --> 00:03:34,300
exposure limit of eighty five A weighted

103
00:03:34,310 --> 00:03:36,030
decibels. For every three decibel

104
00:03:36,190 --> 00:03:37,790
increase, the recommended exposure

105
00:03:37,790 --> 00:03:39,470
duration is cut in half.

106
00:03:39,470 --> 00:03:41,230
At eighty eight decibels, that's four

107
00:03:41,230 --> 00:03:43,470
hours. At ninety one, two hours.

108
00:03:43,470 --> 00:03:45,710
And at one hundred decibels, just fifteen

109
00:03:45,790 --> 00:03:48,190
minutes. OSHA's legal limit for general

110
00:03:48,190 --> 00:03:50,350
industry is less protective: ninety

111
00:03:50,350 --> 00:03:52,430
decibels over eight hours, with exposure

112
00:03:52,430 --> 00:03:54,350
duration halved for every five decibel

113
00:03:54,510 --> 00:03:56,430
increase. These limits are based on long

114
00:03:56,510 --> 00:03:58,430
term hearing risk considerations, not a

115
00:03:58,430 --> 00:04:00,430
guarantee of safety for every individual.

116
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:02,360
Let's return to the machines from the

117
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:04,840
beginning. Using the NIOSH exchange rate

118
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:06,840
formula, the recommended exposure duration

119
00:04:06,840 --> 00:04:09,560
in hours equals eight divided by two

120
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:11,400
raised to the power of the sound level

121
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:14,120
minus eighty five, divided by three.

122
00:04:14,120 --> 00:04:16,040
At eighty decibels, the sound is below

123
00:04:16,040 --> 00:04:18,040
NIOSH's eighty five decibel reference

124
00:04:18,040 --> 00:04:19,560
level for an eight hour workday.

125
00:04:19,560 --> 00:04:21,560
But at ninety decibels, the recommended

126
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:23,640
exposure duration falls to approximately

127
00:04:23,640 --> 00:04:25,080
two and a half hours.

128
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:27,240
A difference of just ten decibels means

129
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:29,320
ten times the sound intensity and a

130
00:04:29,320 --> 00:04:31,560
dramatic reduction in recommended exposure

131
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:32,280
time.

132
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:34,360
Decibels aren't just for sound.

133
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:36,680
Engineers use them for wireless signals,

134
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:39,480
amplifier gain, and electronic noise.

135
00:04:39,480 --> 00:04:41,240
They're useful whenever we need to compare

136
00:04:41,240 --> 00:04:43,560
quantities that can vary across enormous

137
00:04:43,560 --> 00:04:44,920
numerical ranges.

138
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:46,920
A logarithmic scale compresses those

139
00:04:46,920 --> 00:04:48,600
differences into numbers that are much

140
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:49,960
easier to work with.

141
00:04:49,960 --> 00:04:51,640
So here's what to remember.

142
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:54,120
Three decibels more means approximately

143
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:55,800
twice the sound intensity.

144
00:04:55,800 --> 00:04:58,200
Ten decibels more means ten times the

145
00:04:58,200 --> 00:05:00,040
intensity. And over the same amount of

146
00:05:00,040 --> 00:05:02,650
time, ten times the intensity means ten

147
00:05:02,810 --> 00:05:04,810
times the sound energy reaches a given

148
00:05:04,810 --> 00:05:07,450
area. That's why ninety decibels isn't

149
00:05:07,450 --> 00:05:09,130
just a little more than eighty.

150
00:05:09,130 --> 00:05:11,130
It's a tenfold increase in sound

151
00:05:11,130 --> 00:05:12,250
intensity.

152
00:05:12,250 --> 00:05:14,250
If you liked this video, please give it a

153
00:05:14,250 --> 00:05:16,170
like and subscribe for more everyday

154
00:05:16,170 --> 00:05:17,930
engineering. Thanks for watching!
